AB  - Critical thinking is significant in talent cultivation as a form of higher-order thinking. Virtual reality (VR), leveraging its “3I” technology matrix (i.e., Immersion, Interaction, and Imagination), is regarded as a potential tool for fostering critical thinking. Consequently, numerous researchers worldwide have conducted empirical studies focusing on utilising VR-assisted learning to enhance students‘ critical thinking. However, the impact of such interventions remains contentious, with research findings scattered and lacking unified evaluation criteria and methodologies. This meta-analysis of 37 empirical studies from 2014 to February 2026 reveals that the deep integration of VR and pedagogy yields a significant, moderate-to-large positive effect on students’ critical thinking development (Q = 312.14, I² = 88 %, z = 6.23, df = 36, p<0.00001). The findings indicate a low likelihood of publication bias (Egger‘s regression test = 0.067, p > 0.05). Furthermore, the teaching cycle, education level, critical thinking dimension, teaching method, and knowledge domain were found to moderate the overall effect size significantly. Based on this comprehensive assessment, this study proposes a mechanism of action for how VR-assisted learning promotes students’ critical thinking and discusses applicable scenarios for effectively enhancing this ability.
