DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.learninstruc.2026.102393
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959475226000782
KW  - Critical thinking
KW  - Instructional intervention
KW  - Cognitive intervention
KW  - Metacognition
AB  - Background
Critical thinking, the ability to reason analytically, evaluate evidence and resist cognitive bias, has become increasingly essential as students navigate information-rich environments. However, evidence on interventions that promote critical thinking and reveal their neurocognitive mechanisms remains limited.
Aims
This study examined how metacognitive instructional and adaptive cognitive training interventions affect students’ critical thinking and whether they engage distinct neurocognitive patterns.
Sample
Participants were 74 undergraduate students majoring in science and engineering.
Methods
A 3 × 2 mixed pretest–posttest design was employed. Participants were randomly assigned to instructional, cognitive, or control groups. The intervention groups received four weeks of intervention, while the control group received none. Behavioral assessments were conducted at baseline, immediately after the intervention, and at a six-month follow-up. Electroencephalography (EEG) was recorded at baseline and posttest during belief-bias syllogistic reasoning to capture neural changes related to the interventions.
Results
Both interventions led to significant improvements in critical thinking, which were maintained over a six-month follow-up. However, their neural oscillatory patterns diverged: the instructional intervention increased frontocentral beta and decreased parietal alpha power, reflecting enhanced metacognitive regulation and analytical reasoning, whereas the cognitive intervention reduced frontocentral theta and parietal alpha activity, indicating improved processing efficiency and access to working memory.
Implications
The study reveals the neurocognitive mechanisms underlying critical thinking enhancement and underscores the importance of integrating diverse, evidence-based approaches into higher education to cultivate enduring and transferable reasoning skills.
