AB  - Empirical studies of generative AI (GenAI) in architecture education has often emphasized design studios and visual ideation, while comparatively less is known about its use in non-studio history and theory courses, where factual accuracy, verification practices, and cultural representation are central to coursework. Evidence is therefore limited on how architecture students in such courses perceive GenAI's usefulness, learning value, and responsible use in ordinary assessed tasks. This descriptive case study investigates architecture students' perceptions of GenAI in a Contemporary Architecture history and theory course at a public university, drawing on technology-acceptance and learning-oriented perspectives. Fifty-three students completed a post-assignment survey after preparing contemporary architect case-study presentations in which GenAI tools, including large language models and multimodal platforms, could be used optionally. The survey assessed perceived usefulness, ease of use, perceived effects on learning, motivation, creativity, accuracy and trust, ethics and guidance, cultural representation, task–technology fit, satisfaction, and intentions to continue using GenAI, and was complemented by open-ended responses. Exploratory nonparametric analyses examined patterns by prior AI experience and selected demographic variables. Overall, students reported that GenAI improved efficiency, information organization, and perceived presentation quality, while expressing more cautious views about gains in creativity and critical thinking and noting frequent needs for fact-checking. Students with longer prior AI experience tended to report higher ease of use and process usefulness, whereas newer users expressed higher ethical awareness. Open-ended responses emphasized the need for clearer institutional guidance and structured curricular training, alongside concerns about accuracy, bias, and Western-centric defaults. Findings position GenAI in this history and theory context as a helpful but limited support and suggest course-level integration strategies that foreground verification workflows, ethical transparency, and culturally attentive use.
