AB  - Academic integrity has been a salient challenge in higher education since the early emergence of generative AI tools and remains a pressing issue as students and institutions continue to negotiate norms for their ethical academic use. This study tested an AI-adapted educational TAM framework with ethical and learning-risk extensions among early student users of ChatGPT. A total of 500 Croatian university students completed a self-report questionnaire assessing Ease and Reliability of Use, Learning Usefulness and Engagement, Perceived Learning Risks, Ethical Concerns, and Intention to Use ChatGPT for Homework Assignments. MANCOVA revealed significant differences across user profiles. Homework Users reported more favorable evaluations, including higher ease and reliability, learning usefulness and engagement, and intention to use, together with lower perceived risks and ethical concerns. Parallel mediation analyses showed that Learning Usefulness and Engagement was the strongest indirect pathway linking Ease and Reliability of Use with Intention to Use ChatGPT, whereas Ethical Concerns showed a smaller but significant indirect association. Indirect pathways involving Perceived Learning Risks were comparatively weak. Exploratory serial mediation analyses supported a similar pattern, in which higher perceived ease and reliability was associated with greater learning usefulness and engagement, lower ethical concerns, and stronger intentions to use ChatGPT for homework. Overall, the findings suggest that functional, ethical, and risk-related evaluations of generative AI tools are closely associated with students’ intentions toward academic AI use. The study underscores the need for clear institutional guidelines, AI literacy initiatives, and open discussion of responsible AI use in higher education.
